---
import { ACTION_QUERY_PARAMS } from 'astro:actions';
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
---

<MainLayout
  title="The Moments Between | Justin Ho"
  description="A reflection on photography, editing, and the authentic moments that often happen between poses."
>

<article class="journal-post">
  <a href="/journal" class="back-link">← Back to Journal</a>
  <header class="journal-header">
    <p class="journal-label">Journal</p>
    <h1>The Moments Between</h1>
    <p class="journal-meta">13 June 2026 · Photography</p>
    <p class="journal-intro">
      A short reflection on photography, editing, and the quiet moments that often feel the most true.
    </p>
    
  </header>

  <section class="journal-content">
    <p>People often think photography is about timing.</p>

    <p>Being in the right place at the right moment and pressing the shutter.</p>

    <p>There is some truth to that.</p>



    <p>They’re the moments in between.</p>

    <p>The moment between poses.</p>

    <p>The moment after someone has finished speaking.</p>

    <p>The moment before an athlete starts moving.</p>

    <p>The moment after a bride and groom stop looking at the camera.</p>

    <p>When photographing people, I often notice something interesting.</p>

    <p>I’ll ask them to smile.</p>

    <p>They give me a smile.</p>

    <p>It’s perfectly fine.</p>

    <p>Then they relax.</p>

    <p>They laugh because they feel awkward.</p>

    <p>Their shoulders drop.</p>

    <p>Their expression changes.</p>

    <p>For a brief second, they stop trying to look good.</p>

    <p>And that’s usually when I take the photograph.</p>

    <p>Not because posed photographs are bad.</p>

    <p>But because the moments between poses often reveal something more authentic.</p>

    <p>I see the same thing when editing documentaries.</p>

<p>The line that makes it into the final film is important.</p>
<p>But sometimes the most revealing moment is the pause before the line.</p>
<p>Or the smile afterwards.</p>
<p>Or the silence that follows.</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>The camera records events.<br />
      The editor searches for meaning.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>Both are really looking for the same thing.</p>

    <p>Moments that reveal something true.</p>

    <p>Perhaps that’s why I’ve always been drawn to documentary storytelling.</p>

    <p>
      Whether I’m photographing a wedding, filming an interview, editing a corporate story
      or simply walking around with a camera, I’m rarely looking for perfection.
    </p>

    <p>I’m looking for the moments between.</p>

    <p>The moments when people stop performing and start being themselves.</p>
  </section>
</article>
</MainLayout>

